      "knowledge_point": "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/ce3aec85d418c8f51988f819c04403f3f3ea2e0c0e35165ed729fc1e78d0f12f.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of an artwork $y,$ in thousands of dollars, and the number of decades $x$ since it was first created. The equation of the trend line is $y=3x+5.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the artwork to be worth $$32\\,000.$",
      "worked_solution": "To predict the time that must pass before the artwork is worth $$32\\,000,$ we need to substitute $y=32$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+5 \\\\ 32 & =3x+5 \\\\ 3x & =27 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes approximately $9$ decades for the artwork to have a value of $$32\\,000.$ \n\nNote: The point where $y=32$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable.",
