  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892",
    "id": "q-133943",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-133943",
      "knowledge_point_id": "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650",
      "knowledge_point": "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/7ba7aa1cf49bb5a2dc430d9a21baaa8491aa61d8898f2aad142406c5c569b51b.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $x$ when $y=7.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,4).$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/59efe1b79b0d032179a5210ee694b0d59ad082853ca9fb80a9d79d9f6444f2bf.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.75$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.75x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.75x+b \\\\ 1 & =0.75(1)+b \\\\ 1 & =0.75+b \\\\ b & =0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.75x+0.25.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=7$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.75x+0.25 \\\\ 7 & =0.75x+0.25 \\\\ 6.75 & =0.75x \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=7$ we have $x≈9.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=7$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable.",
